#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:1
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.<name>.url"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:9
msgid ""
"The URL for a submodule. This variable is copied from the .gitmodules file "
"to the git config via 'git submodule init'. The user can change the "
"configured URL before obtaining the submodule via 'git submodule update'. If "
"neither submodule.<name>.active nor submodule.active are set, the presence "
"of this variable is used as a fallback to indicate whether the submodule is "
"of interest to git commands.  See linkgit:git-submodule[1] and "
"linkgit:gitmodules[5] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.<name>.update"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:19
msgid ""
"The method by which a submodule is updated by 'git submodule update', which "
"is the only affected command, others such as 'git checkout "
"--recurse-submodules' are unaffected. It exists for historical reasons, when "
"'git submodule' was the only command to interact with submodules; settings "
"like `submodule.active` and `pull.rebase` are more specific. It is populated "
"by `git submodule init` from the linkgit:gitmodules[5] file.  See "
"description of 'update' command in linkgit:git-submodule[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.<name>.branch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:25
msgid ""
"The remote branch name for a submodule, used by `git submodule update "
"--remote`.  Set this option to override the value found in the `.gitmodules` "
"file.  See linkgit:git-submodule[1] and linkgit:gitmodules[5] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:26
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.<name>.fetchRecurseSubmodules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:32
msgid ""
"This option can be used to control recursive fetching of this submodule. It "
"can be overridden by using the --[no-]recurse-submodules command-line option "
"to \"git fetch\" and \"git pull\".  This setting will override that from in "
"the linkgit:gitmodules[5] file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.<name>.ignore"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:48
msgid ""
"Defines under what circumstances \"git status\" and the diff family show a "
"submodule as modified. When set to \"all\", it will never be considered "
"modified (but it will nonetheless show up in the output of status and commit "
"when it has been staged), \"dirty\" will ignore all changes to the "
"submodule's work tree and takes only differences between the HEAD of the "
"submodule and the commit recorded in the superproject into "
"account. \"untracked\" will additionally let submodules with modified "
"tracked files in their work tree show up.  Using \"none\" (the default when "
"this option is not set) also shows submodules that have untracked files in "
"their work tree as changed.  This setting overrides any setting made in "
".gitmodules for this submodule, both settings can be overridden on the "
"command line by using the \"--ignore-submodules\" option. The 'git "
"submodule' commands are not affected by this setting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:49
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.<name>.active"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:54
msgid ""
"Boolean value indicating if the submodule is of interest to git commands.  "
"This config option takes precedence over the submodule.active config "
"option. See linkgit:gitsubmodules[7] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:55
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.active"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:59
msgid ""
"A repeated field which contains a pathspec used to match against a "
"submodule's path to determine if the submodule is of interest to git "
"commands. See linkgit:gitsubmodules[7] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:60
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.recurse"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:63
msgid ""
"A boolean indicating if commands should enable the `--recurse-submodules` "
"option by default. Defaults to false."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:71
msgid ""
"When set to true, it can be deactivated via the `--no-recurse-submodules` "
"option. Note that some Git commands lacking this option may call some of the "
"above commands affected by `submodule.recurse`; for instance `git remote "
"update` will call `git fetch` but does not have a `--no-recurse-submodules` "
"option.  For these commands a workaround is to temporarily change the "
"configuration value by using `git -c submodule.recurse=0`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:75
msgid ""
"The following list shows the commands that accept `--recurse-submodules` and "
"whether they are supported by this setting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:78
msgid ""
"`checkout`, `fetch`, `grep`, `pull`, `push`, `read-tree`, `reset`, `restore` "
"and `switch` are always supported."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:79
msgid "`clone` and `ls-files` are not supported."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:81
msgid "`branch` is supported only if `submodule.propagateBranches` is enabled"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.propagateBranches"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:89
msgid ""
"[EXPERIMENTAL] A boolean that enables branching support when using "
"`--recurse-submodules` or `submodule.recurse=true`.  Enabling this will "
"allow certain commands to accept `--recurse-submodules` and certain commands "
"that already accept `--recurse-submodules` will now consider branches.  "
"Defaults to false."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:90
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.fetchJobs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:95
msgid ""
"Specifies how many submodules are fetched/cloned at the same time.  A "
"positive integer allows up to that number of submodules fetched in "
"parallel. A value of 0 will give some reasonable default.  If unset, it "
"defaults to 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:96
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.alternateLocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:102
msgid ""
"Specifies how the submodules obtain alternates when submodules are "
"cloned. Possible values are `no`, `superproject`.  By default `no` is "
"assumed, which doesn't add references. When the value is set to "
"`superproject` the submodule to be cloned computes its alternates location "
"relative to the superprojects alternate."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:103
#, no-wrap
msgid "submodule.alternateErrorStrategy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/config/submodule.txt:108
msgid ""
"Specifies how to treat errors with the alternates for a submodule as "
"computed via `submodule.alternateLocation`. Possible values are `ignore`, "
"`info`, `die`. Default is `die`. Note that if set to `ignore` or `info`, and "
"if there is an error with the computed alternate, the clone proceeds as if "
"no alternate was specified."
msgstr ""
